WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
How can white flowers disappear, then return in the next generation?

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.920
Crossing a true-breeding purple pea plant with a white one gives only purple

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.380
offspring. Later, white flowers return among their descendants.

4
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.930
The flower-color gene comes in different versions, called alleles.

5
00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.510
We will track a purple allele and a white allele.

6
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.070
Each plant carries a pair.

7
00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:32.563
The original purple parent carried only purple alleles.

8
00:00:33.743 --> 00:00:35.963
The white parent carried only white alleles.

9
00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:39.703
Their offspring received one allele from each parent.

10
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:42.757
That allele pair is the genotype.

11
00:00:43.957 --> 00:00:46.337
The visible flower color is the phenotype.

12
00:00:47.657 --> 00:00:50.337
This plant looks purple, yet it still carries white.

13
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.957
Matching allele pairs are homozygous.

14
00:00:55.057 --> 00:00:56.797
A mixed pair is heterozygous.

15
00:00:57.877 --> 00:01:02.997
The first-generation plants are heterozygous, which explains how the white allele

16
00:01:02.997 --> 00:01:03.997
stays present.

17
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.250
Here, the purple allele is dominant, so one copy makes purple flowers.

18
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:15.350
The white allele is recessive, so white appears only when no purple allele is

19
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.350
present.

20
00:01:16.783 --> 00:01:21.443
Dominant does not mean common, stronger, or more likely to be passed on.

21
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:26.003
It describes which phenotype appears when the two alleles differ.

22
00:01:27.117 --> 00:01:31.397
When a plant makes sex cells, its allele pair separates during meiosis.

23
00:01:32.857 --> 00:01:37.277
A heterozygous plant therefore makes both kinds in about equal numbers.

24
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.220
Fertilization brings one sex cell from each parent together.

25
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.820
Which sex cells meet is a matter of chance.

26
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.500
A grid can organize every possible pairing.

27
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:56.083
Because each parent makes both sex-cell types equally often, every box has the same

28
00:01:56.083 --> 00:02:00.443
probability. The grid's area becomes a probability map.

29
00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:03.507
Both parents can send either allele.

30
00:02:04.927 --> 00:02:06.887
Predict which pairing can make a white plant.

31
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.567
Watch the allele cards before the flowers appear.

32
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.050
White appears only when a small p arrives from both parents.

33
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.030
The other pairings carry a dominant allele, so their flowers are purple.

34
00:02:26.183 --> 00:02:28.283
Read genotype before phenotype.

35
00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:31.543
The genotype pattern has three groups.

36
00:02:32.863 --> 00:02:37.363
Complete dominance combines the first two groups into the same purple phenotype.

37
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:41.827
The grid predicts the genotype pattern shown below it.

38
00:02:42.987 --> 00:02:46.027
Grouping by visible color gives the phenotype pattern.

39
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:50.107
Both describe chances across repeated offspring.

40
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.240
These four boxes are equally likely outcomes, not four promised offspring.

41
00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.440
A small batch may miss white entirely.

42
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.460
Another batch may contain several.

43
00:03:03.533 --> 00:03:06.373
Alleles separate, and sex cells meet by chance.

44
00:03:07.573 --> 00:03:09.353
The square organizes those chances.

45
00:03:10.693 --> 00:03:12.933
It does not promise which offspring will arrive.
